e ah eight hours  hi I'm Serge and I helped develop Gmail  voice and video chat from Sweden our  team works closely with a team in the US  so video chat comes in really handy some  things are just better communicated  face-to-face where you can see  expressions gestures and I can point  things out on a diagram plus whenever I  travel I get to see my kids so let me  show you how this works gmail voice and  video chat is integrated right into  Gmail so it's very easy to use to start  a voice or video chat simply click on  the video and more link at the bottom of  the chat window when a person receives a  call they'll hear a ring and they'll see  a notification that they can choose to  answer you can pop out the chat window  and adjust the size and positioning as  you like you can even make it go  fullscreen through a single click it's  free and easy to use  visit gmail.com slash video chat to get  started if ever someone asks you to  volunteer for something like that  think twice what's important here so I  had been working with software video  conferencing for 10 years so I started  around 97 and we had a small company and  in 10 years we were able to get 200  thousand users and we were all proud  when we launched this thing the two  hundred thousand user mark passed in a  few hours it is amazing the scale at  which  Google operates and having all these  users with all these different firewall  settings and all these different  networks and all these different cameras  and audio card and trying to support all  of them was an amazing challenge but we  learned a ton from it  so building at that scale helped us  understand how to how to cater to many  many many scenarios let's see if I can  go forward here no I'm sorry about that  I need to click on the white area  perfect  oops so we started from with Gmail video  chat and then we went to whoops the  video started playing in the background  please don't make me hear myself again  and this was all fine one-on-one  conversations but we wanted to replace a  Tandberg experience we wanted to give  group video meetings so we have an  engineer in in Sweden and a UX designer  who was based out of our Kirkland office  Jake Knapp they had been thinking a lot  about this and it was pretty obvious  very very quickly that we wouldn't be  able to do full-blown group video  conferencing inside of Gmail so we did a  little we did a little sandbox play area  where we started to play with with the  software in different ways and these two  individuals came to the Epiphany that  hey why don't we just tie an actual  meeting or a meeting room to our URL and  so we built a prototype and we called it  go meet and say hey let's go meet infu  go meet foo and that caught on and  people thought hey this is neat great  and this led to something called go meet  that we were using internally at Google  in 2009 and this is another prototype  Arthur has not aged lucky him and you'll  see some of the people on that screen  are in the room with us today  and we launched this to Google in 2009  and it became it picked up it became a  big success people could work from home  the latency was a lot lower than those  video bridges that had to transcode  there was no transcoding in this system  and people could take meetings from  their desk they couldn't take meetings  from home they could take meetings on  the road and so there was a there was a  moment where this started to grow  quicker and the Tandberg rooms were  being used less and less and this became  Google Hangouts a little bit later on  one thing that's that was different with  this back in 2009 is video is on by  default you went into a meeting go meet  hang out or go meet foo and video was on  and that was an important pillar for  this software experience because if you  have if all these people would join and  you'd all be sort of avatars and the  audio-only who's the first person to  dare show themselves on camera in front  of all these others right who's that  conceited person that's going to be the  first one to say hey I'm so great I'm  gonna show myself for everyone and so  those system didn't work the ones that  didn't have video on by default straight  from the start they don't work for  building team culture this created it  created a sense of team and after a few  times you don't care about your hair or  the mess the kids made in the background  because if this is your team this is  your safe space these are the people you  create with this is a people you build  software with our second innovation  track was about the stock building video  communication software was a real pain  back then you had to license third party  software not just surf third party  technology but a third party audio stack  a third party video stack and then you  had the fun part of taking to two  different technology  from two different companies put them on  top of your own Network stock and then  fit this into a plugin remember the  plug-in architecture is called NPAPI the  N stands for Netscape just this is  something people forget and you needed  the plug-in for Mac Windows and Linux  and you needed to have a great flow for  deploying these plugins but also for  keeping them updated so how do you  update a plug-in how do you download the  plug-in so it doesn't affect the call  how do you update the plug-in so you  don't tear down a call that's going on  and how do you do that in a browser  without the actual software page being  showed up because that's usually where  these things were get triggered very  tricky and with one of the best install  flows we had that you could find on the  Internet we still had first first  attempts to join a meeting failures of  about 30% people just cannot it's just  too hard to download software on your  desktop and find that file run the  Installer completely installer remember  that restart the browser because this is  a plugin people would fail at joining  their meetings a first time all this  complexity of deployment and having to  integrate other people and other  companies technology meant that  innovation stagnated and quality was a  hard thing to improve when you're not in  control of your own stack so the team at  Google started a project called WebRTC  WebRTC is an open source audio and video  platform that developers can use to  create video and audio communication  apps it's available on Chrome Android  and iOS and there's other browsers that  take the code and implement it in their  own browsers  it's a open source community effort with  different standard bodies it's highly  secure and in chrome this is a one-click  experience there's no more download you  click and you're in your meeting that  helped a lot of users  and it's not a stretch of the  imagination to say that WebRTC today  powers video conferencing on the  Internet  if use Google Hangouts Google duo  facebook Messenger GoToMeeting Twilio  the list of companies is over a thousand  long of companies that integrate WebRTC  the code that Google wrote and Google  made available for creating web for  creating video conferencing experiences  so now we had started building software  then we decided that we wanted to own  this stock we wanted to understand how  audio and video works we wanted to make  that part better but we still had Google  Chrome  growing like crazy in all these meeting  rooms that needed video conferencing  support and so this is a video of two of  my colleague Steve Taurus and Eric ng we  bought a HP TouchSmart in 2008 and  that's the one we bought this ran  Windows Vista it ran Firefox and  something called grease monkey if any of  you remember this it would grab the  video chat little bubble that you saw in  that video and it would make it big so  that you didn't know gmail was there in  the background and with the touch screen  it allowed you to punch in a meeting  name it cost $1000 as a thousand 50  instead of several thousands of dollars  there were no remote controls or devices  this was an all-in-one and so this was  our first attempt at creating a video  conferencing room set up by ourselves  I will project a kiosk a PC to function  as a tamper replacement so I'm gonna  go ahead and part on digs you hit dial  it will go directly to this page that  will show you a video conference that  you'll join in progress this was on the  first time if it all worked just like  talk video and that was it the actual  meeting the actual videos like 15  minutes long because it was powered by  this time it took so much time to boot  but so you know the team did there was  so much work that needed to be done  after this first proof-of-concept right  we move this to Linux we redid the  front-end we we we had to do fleet  management on this so that we because we  wanted to deploy thousands of these but  that little video those two individuals  and the others that worked with them led  to a revolution at Google we built a  video conferencing appliance and so this  is today at Google and all of our  meeting rooms have video conferencing  and they're all built on top of the same  software that I just talked about  hangouts and WebRTC it's powered by by  computers that run that run in Chrome OS  today we're going to talk about that in  a little minute but we've got over  15,000 rooms today that have video  conferencing capabilities so this is one  of their offices in Tel Aviv here's an  office in London this is a smaller  huddle room and this is how good we've  become at choosing the cables passing  the cabling the amount of discipline and  thought that has gone through all of  this is just staggering because when you  have to do something  15,000 times across 70 offices now or 70  countries now  you can't let anything to chance so what  cables are you use how do you pull the  cables what holes you drill  what boxes that you choose to insulate  sound from the PC and all those things  are really really important and we put  that ubiquity to use 